  "question": {
    "text": "In which one of the following metal carbonyls, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>CO</mi></math> forms a bridge between metal atoms?"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><msub><mi>Co</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>CO</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>8</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": " <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><msub><mi>Mn</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>CO</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>10</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><msub><mi>Os</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>CO</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><msub><mi>Ru</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>CO</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "A"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>In the most common bridging mode, denoted <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> or simply <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ</mi></math>, the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>CO</mi></math>  ligand bridges a pair of metals. This bonding mode is observed in the commonly available metal carbonyls: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Co</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mfenced><mi>CO</mi></mfenced><mn>8</mn></msub></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Fe</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mfenced><mi>CO</mi></mfenced><mn>9</mn></msub></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Fe</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mfenced><mi>CO</mi></mfenced><mn>12</mn></msub></math>and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Co</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><msub><mfenced><mi>CO</mi></mfenced><mn>12</mn></msub></math>. In certain higher nuclearity clusters, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>CO</mi></math> bridges between three or even four metals.</p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0094_927b13c57f1b.png\" style=\"width: 380px; height: 345px;\"/></p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0094_55171a3ef73c.png\" style=\"width: 400px; height: 526px;\"/></p>"
  }
}